  "enhanced_information": "The 4-year-old boy presents with periorbital and lower extremity edema, significant proteinuria (4+), and low serum albumin (2.6 g/dL), which are indicative of nephrotic syndrome. The absence of hematuria and normal serum complement levels further support this diagnosis, as they are more consistent with nephrotic syndrome than post-infectious glomerulonephritis. Prednisone is the first-line treatment for nephrotic syndrome in children, as it helps reduce proteinuria and manage edema. Therefore, the most appropriate next step in management is to initiate prednisone therapy."
